t some of  the big ones sent around the idea that  it causes neurological damage or even  autism now the thinking behind these  theories is that the glutamate from the  msg enters our bloodstream and then gets  into our brain  leading to neurological damage this is  actually physiologically impossible  because msg can't even cross the blood  brain barrier where it would do harm  it's also been cited that msg causes  headaches or kind of like hangover like  experiences  symptoms that are often racistly deemed  chinese restaurant syndrome  now not sure that has aged well here in  2021 but problematic moniker aside there  actually is no scientifically  established link between msg and  migraine headaches in fact the  international headache society removed  it from its list of migraine triggers in  2018. obviously i do think that all  experiences are valid so i don't want to  say no you don't get a headache when you  eat msg-related takeout but i'm just  sharing what the science says and there  is evidence actually to suggest that a  lot of those transient symptoms like  headache flushing tingling palpitations  drowsiness and numbness  aren't actually evident in most people  when they don't know they're eating msg  so this is what we call the nocebo  effect and it definitely is very real  having said that we also know that the  fda has observed that some people can in  fact experience these mild transient  symptoms though their standard for this  claim is actually a mega dose of three  grams all at once without food so to put  that into perspective to get that  equivalent in food you would have to  drink over a cup of soy sauce or eat  nine ounces of parmesan cheese i love  cheese sounds like catherine loves  cheese but that's a lot of cheese so if  msg doesn't feel good to you then by all  means please do avoid it i think we all  know our bodies best but generally  speaking i'm just stating that the  evidence does not support the common  concern that msg is poison or  